{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>For Your salvation do I hope, O YHWH.</b> Because a horse that bites at the heel of its rider's horse may be struck by its rider lest it crush his head with his sword, therefore the prophet said, For Your salvation do I hope, O YHWH. The kaf is directed toward Dan. The meaning of 'I have hoped, O HaShem, that Your salvation may come to be,' or the noun stands in place of the verbal noun, and the meaning is 'to save You.' Similarly, 'Let us rejoice in Your salvation' (Psalms 20:6) — if the kaf is directed toward David or the Messiah his son. The Nagid says that the word 'and he said' is lacking, and so it should read: and he said, For Your salvation do I hope, O YHWH. R. Isaac said: When the prophet saw Dan his son in the form of a serpent in the vision of prophecy, Jacob was immediately afraid and said, Save me, O YHWH."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {0 "The word הכ״ף (the letter kaf) refers to the prefixed lamed/kaf pronominal element; translated literally as 'the kaf.' 'Bites at the heel of its rider's horse' is uncertain — the Hebrew syntax of סוס יפחד מרוכב הסוס is difficult and possibly corrupt; rendered hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:48:34.056894499Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}